      en: "The Moma Titanium Minerals Mine, situated on the remote northern coast of Nampula Province, is one of the world's largest deposits of heavy mineral sands. Operated by the Irish company Kenmare Resources, the mine uses massive floating dredges in artificial ponds to extract ilmenite, rutile, and zircon from the ancient coastal dunes. These minerals are critical raw materials for manufacturing titanium dioxide pigment, used globally in paints, plastics, and paper. The facility operates its own dedicated coastal jetty, allowing the processed heavy minerals to be loaded directly onto ocean-going bulk carriers. Geography K8 — Heavy mineral sands, coastal dune mining, and global supply chains."
